{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC verification against merged `a26a73277c8a`: 5 MET, 4 PARTIAL, 1 NOT MET\n\nVerified each criterion against the merged code rather than ticking on the merge. Read-only; no\n`mutmut run` executed.\n\n**Genuinely delivered (MET).** AC3 is the important one and it holds properly: the enforcement\ntests RUN a probe and assert on filesystem state afterward (`test_mutation_sandbox.py:71-79`\noutside-deny asserting `not target.exists()`, `:83-92` inside-succeeds, `:96-108` reproducing the\nincident's own `rm -rf <protected>/*` shape). The argv-only tests are separately quarantined at\n`:264-301` and correctly labelled as binding checks. On this macOS host all 28 tests passed with\nZERO skips — real Seatbelt enforcement observed on a laptop. AC4, AC5 (no `unshare` fallback\nanywhere — confirmed by repo-wide grep), AC7, AC9 and AC10 also hold.\n\n**AC6 — NOT MET, and this is the one that matters.** The Linux write-deny is not proven on Linux.\n`_build-and-test.yml:356-361` does install bubblewrap, but the merged code's OWN comment\n(`mutation_sandbox.py:113-115`) records that GitHub's ubuntu runners ship bwrap while denying\nunprivileged user namespaces (`bwrap: setting up uid map: Permission denied`), so the functional\nprobe correctly reports no mechanism. `probe()` returns `None`, the `@live` skipif at\n`test_mutation_sandbox.py:61-64` fires, and all four enforcement tests SKIP on ubuntu-latest —\nsilently under `-q`. So `Verified +1` does not distinguish 'ran' from 'skipped', which is exactly\nwhat this criterion was written to prevent. Two commits also contradict each other here:\n`b63cf799ed90` installed bwrap specifically so these would not skip, and `a26a73277c8a` documents\nthat they still find no mechanism.\n\n**AC1 and AC8 — PARTIAL, and I am NOT going to quietly reword them.** The implementation added a\n`CI` branch (`mutation_sandbox.py:192-202`) that waives the sandbox entirely when no mechanism\nexists on a CI runner, with a reasoned WARNING: a CI runner is already the disposable environment\nthe sandbox substitutes for, and the requirement exists to protect WORKSTATIONS. I think that\nreasoning is sound, and it matters that the incident this ticket exists for happened on a\nWORKSTATION — where this path still aborts or sandboxes. But the ACs as WRITTEN say the run\naborts when no mechanism is available, full stop, and they no longer describe the code. AC1 also\nnames the venv in the allow-list while `mutation_gate.py:594-596` deliberately excludes it\n(`docs/local-dev-env.md:130` still says otherwise — doc/code drift).\n\n**AC2 and AC11 — PARTIAL.** AC2's deny is tripped against a `mutmut run` argv SHAPE, not a real\nmutmut invocation; the real call site is bound only by AST inspection. AC11's runtime half holds\n(`sandbox_env` is applied to both subprocesses at `mutation_gate.py:597`) but the only test\nasserts the RETURNED MAPPING, never a child's actual `HOME`, and `sandbox_env` has no call-site\nbinding test — deleting `mutation_gate.py:597` leaves the whole suite green. That is the same\ndefect class the author explicitly guarded `wrap` against.\n\nNot closing. Splitting the residue into follow-ups so the delivered work is not held hostage to\nit, per the operator's standing allowance to split as long as the work stays tracked."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787855367135487001,"uuid":"25812838-07b3-4430-ae70-263d1d698854"}